+## Why NCP exists when MUSIC exists
+
+[MUSIC](https://ebrains.eu/tools/music) is an API for MPI-based neuronal
+simulators to exchange event and continuous data during one coordinated
+multi-simulation. Participating programs link the MUSIC library, share its
+communicator, and advance simulated time through runtime ticks.
+[NEST's MUSIC tutorial](https://nest-simulator.readthedocs.io/en/stable/tutorials/music_tutorial/music_tutorial_3.html)
+shows that coordinated runtime model. NCP addresses a different boundary: a
+versioned network contract between a neural service, a body, a simulator, and
+read-only observers that can be deployed, restarted, upgraded, and secured
+independently.
+
+NCP therefore adds explicit wire identity, authenticated roles, session
+generations, authority leases, stream positions, deadlines, idempotent lifecycle
+operations, plant-profile binding, and bounded failure behavior. These protocol
+functions complement MUSIC, ROS, and Zenoh. NCP does not replace their scheduling,
+device, or transport functions. NEST remains behind an adapter or gateway instead
+of acquiring NCP transport logic in its scientific kernel.
+
+<picture>
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="docs/diagrams/purpose-dark.svg">
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" srcset="docs/diagrams/purpose-light.svg">
+  <img alt="Informative boundary map for the unreleased, release-blocked 1.0.0-rc.1 candidate. MUSIC owns coordinated MPI co-simulation. NCP owns the typed network-role contract, exact identity, session, authority, replay, and failure semantics. Zenoh, ROS, and deployment adapters provide transport or device integration without granting NCP authority. NEST remains behind an explicit adapter. Direct production-secure Zenoh and ecosystem qualification are not available." src="docs/diagrams/purpose-light.svg" width="900">
+</picture>
+
+Read the diagram by ownership, not by product order. MUSIC can coordinate a
+MUSIC-aware simulation behind an adapter. A transport can carry an NCP frame, and
+a robotics stack can integrate a device. Neither fact supplies NCP role authority,
+body admission, or physical-effect evidence. The adapter must preserve the
+boundary explicitly instead of copying protocol definitions into the scientific
+kernel or consumer project.
+
 ## System at a glance
 
+<picture>
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="docs/diagrams/closed-loop-dark.svg">
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" srcset="docs/diagrams/closed-loop-light.svg">
+  <img alt="Informative causal closed-loop model for the unreleased, release-blocked 1.0.0-rc.1 candidate. A body samples plant state, an Engram or NEST controller target derives a proposal, and NCP binds the proposal to the exact sample. A Crebain body target evaluates remote admission and any independent body-local restriction. Failed remote admission selects no remote action. A dashed boundary separates the selected software action from the deployment-owned physical input. The next accepted sample closes the cycle. A Prisoma target is a read-only observation sink and has no action edge. The reference in-process loop is tested, but the installed ecosystem loop, live security, stability, physical safety, performance, and zero-lag claims are not established." src="docs/diagrams/closed-loop-light.svg" width="1200">
+</picture>
+
+The maintained architecture also includes a
+[publication-scale invariant ledger](docs/implementation/NCP_1_0_LOW_OVERHEAD_ARCHITECTURE.md#closed-loop-causal-model).
+It separates the exact identity, timing, admission, selection, and physical-effect
+equations from the overview so both layers remain readable.
+
+The causal unit is a cycle, not a one-way message. Let `x[k]` be the plant state,
+`y[k]` the sampled sensor value, `Λ[k]` its exact prepared source declaration, and
+`Σ[k] = (Λ[k], seq[k], digest[k])` its full retained publication coordinate. The
+declaration binds the realm, session generation, literal route, verified publisher,
+frame class, security activation, and stream epoch. The wire command carries only
+the compact projection `source = (epoch[k], seq[k])`. Its prepared action binding
+and the body-retained publication record must resolve that projection to one full
+`Σ[k]`. Remote admission and body-local restriction are separate decisions. The
+software selection can be the admitted Active proposal, an installed HOLD or ESTOP
+action, an independently selected body-local action, or no new action. Failed
+remote admission selects no remote action. A simultaneous body-local restriction
+has its own installed source and separate evidence. NCP does not prove that the
+physical plant input equals the selected software action. A feedback edge closes
+only when a deployment transition attributable to the body disposition produces
+`x[k+1]` and the body accepts its causally later sample. Arrival order or temporal
+proximity cannot substitute for exact identity.
+
+Prisoma is on a read-only observation edge. Its analysis can inform a later,
+human-reviewed, content-addressed configuration and a fresh session. It cannot
+change the current tick. The in-process reference Zenoh integration test exercises
+a bounded controller-and-reference-plant cycle with a content-addressed reference
+plant-profile check. It does not exercise the authenticated complete body predicate
+or produce a durable body disposition receipt. Native Engram, Crebain, and Prisoma
+qualification remains **NOT RUN** because their dependency-ready migration tasks
+are still blocked.
+
+The diagram distinguishes three timing boundaries. `loop_latency_ms` covers local
+controller-and-governor computation only. Body-clock admission and terminal
+software-disposition intervals remain separate. End-to-end physical response and
+control stability require deployment measurements and a plant model. NCP does not
+infer either from protocol success.
+
