+## Closed-loop causal model
+
+<picture>
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="../diagrams/closed-loop-dark.svg">
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" srcset="../diagrams/closed-loop-light.svg">
+  <img alt="Informative causal closed-loop model for the unreleased, release-blocked 1.0.0-rc.1 candidate. A body samples plant state, an Engram or NEST controller target derives a proposal, and NCP binds the declared source to the exact sample. A Crebain body target evaluates remote admission and any independent body-local restriction. Failed remote admission selects no remote action. A dashed boundary separates the selected software action from the deployment-owned physical input. A body disposition, attributable deployment transition, and causally later accepted sample close the loop. Prisoma is a read-only sink. The installed ecosystem loop and its security, stability, safety, and performance evidence are NOT RUN." src="../diagrams/closed-loop-light.svg" width="1200">
+</picture>
+
+A system diagram must remain readable at normal document scale. The first plate
+shows the causal path and the body-owned decision boundary. The companion plate
+separates the exact identities and equations from that path. This separation
+keeps the overview legible without hiding the formal constraints.
+
+<picture>
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" srcset="../diagrams/closed-loop-invariants-dark.svg">
+  <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" srcset="../diagrams/closed-loop-invariants-light.svg">
+  <img alt="Informative closed-loop invariant ledger for the unreleased, release-blocked 1.0.0-rc.1 candidate. Four panels separate exact source identity, causal receiver-clock timing, body-owned remote admission, independent local selection, and the plant, observer, and physical-effect boundary. A notation band defines every compact coordinate and dynamics symbol. The equations are design constraints. They are not stability, measured-latency, physical-effect, release, or certification evidence." src="../diagrams/closed-loop-invariants-light.svg" width="1200">
+</picture>
+
+A loop is causal only when a body disposition contributes to a plant transition
+that appears in a later sample. A sensor-to-command exchange is one forward path.
+It is not a complete feedback cycle. The five numbered steps in the figure make
+that distinction explicit.
+
+Let $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$ be a control-cycle index. Let $x_k\in\mathcal{X}$
+be deployment-owned plant state at body sample time $\tau_k^B$. The superscript
+$B$ identifies one body-owned monotonic clock incarnation. The realized sample
+period is:
+
+$$
+\Delta_k = \tau_{k+1}^B - \tau_k^B.
+$$
+
+The value $\Delta_k$ becomes known only when sample $k+1$ exists. An admitted
+cycle requires it to be finite and strictly positive. A zero or negative value
+is a clock or ordering fault. It cannot establish a fast loop. Let the
+deployment select a bounded sensor-disturbance set
+$\mathcal{W}_s$. Let $w_k^s\in\mathcal{W}_s$, and let $h$ be the installed
+sampling function. The observed value is:
+
+$$
+y_k = h(x_k,w_k^s).
+$$
+
+NCP does not define $h$. The deployment defines its channels, units, arities,
+ranges, and plant profile. NCP preserves those declarations and rejects a frame
+that cannot satisfy them.
+
+The sample owns a full exact source coordinate:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+\Lambda_k &= (r,s,g,\ell_k,p_k,f_k,a_k,e_k), \\
+\Sigma_k &= (\Lambda_k,q_k,c_k), \\
+\pi_{\mathrm{source}}(\Sigma_k) &= (e_k,q_k).
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+Here, $\Lambda_k$ is the exact prepared source declaration. It binds authority
+realm $r$, logical session $s$, live generation $g$, literal source route
+$\ell_k$, verified publisher principal $p_k$, frame class and layout $f_k$,
+installed authorization/security activation digest $a_k$, and declared source
+epoch $e_k$. The position $q_k$ is strictly increasing inside that declaration.
+The value $c_k$ is the retained canonical publication digest. The projection
+$\pi_{\mathrm{source}}$ is the exact `StreamPosition` carried by
+`CommandFrame.source`. The prepared action declaration identifies $\Lambda_k$.
+The body must resolve $(\Lambda_k,q_k)$ to one retained publication record and
+compare $c_k$ and the typed source content exactly. The wire projection alone is
+not the full equality coordinate. Arrival time, queue order, a latest value, or a
+nearby timestamp cannot substitute for the retained record.
+This proves the command's declared source identity. It does not prove that opaque
+controller code used that sample to calculate the proposal.
+
+Let $E$ map the admitted typed sample to controller input. Let $z_k$ be the
+controller state. Let $F$ be one prepared controller transition, and let $D$
+decode controller state into a proposal. The controller cannot use the future
+realized interval $\Delta_k$ at decision $k$. Define its available interval as:
+
+$$
+\delta_k^{\mathrm{ctl}} =
+\begin{cases}
+\Delta_{\mathrm{init}}, & k=0, \\
+\tau_k^B-\tau_{k-1}^B=\Delta_{k-1}, & k\geq1,
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+where $\Delta_{\mathrm{init}}>0$ is prepared and bound to the controller
+profile. Both body ticks in the second branch must be finite and belong to the
+same clock incarnation. A missing prior interval, a clock cut, or an unavailable
+checked difference rejects or invokes the installed restrictive policy. It does
+not cause the controller to guess a period. The causal controller step is:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+\hat y_k &= E(y_k), \\
+z_{k+1} &= F(z_k,\hat y_k,\delta_k^{\mathrm{ctl}}), \\
+u_k^\star &= D(z_{k+1}).
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+The controller can be a deterministic reflex, a NEST-backed model, or another
+qualified implementation. Protocol success does not validate $F$, $D$, a paper
+reproduction, or a trained model. A closed-loop command must carry
+`command.source = π_source(Σ[k])` and the exact source time. Its prepared action
+binding must name $\Lambda_k$, and its route/session envelope must equal the
+corresponding members of that declaration. The command stream has its own epoch
+and position. Source and command positions are not equated.
+
+Let the body-owned common remote-admission predicate be:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+B_k ={}& A_k^{\mathrm{raw}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{auth}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{manifest}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{wire}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{session}} \\
+&\land A_k^{\mathrm{actor}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{security}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{profile}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{executor}}.
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+The terms require bounded raw structure, verified ingress, an installed
+default-deny manifest, accepted wire identity, the current session, the verified
+actor, current security state, a matching plant profile, and reserved executor
+capacity. Every common term must hold. Unknown or missing terms evaluate to
+false.
+
+Let $\chi_k$ be the declared source policy. Define:
+
+$$
+A_k^{\mathrm{source}}(\chi_k)=
+\begin{cases}
+\mathrm{true}, & \chi_k=\mathrm{open\mbox{-}loop}, \\
+\text{exact retained correlation to }\Sigma_k,
+  & \chi_k=\mathrm{source\mbox{-}bound}.
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+The closed mode gate is:
+
+$$
+G_k(m_k)=
+\begin{cases}
+A_k^{\mathrm{stream}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{grant,active}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{shape,active}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{lease}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{source}}(\chi_k)
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{safety}}, & m_k=\mathrm{Active}, \\
+A_k^{\mathrm{stream}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{grant,hold}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{lease}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{shape,hold}},
+  & m_k=\mathrm{Hold}, \\
+A_k^{\mathrm{stream}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{grant,estop}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{shape,estop}}, & m_k=\mathrm{ESTOP}, \\
+\mathrm{false}, & \text{otherwise}.
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+Each grant term includes the applicable body-issued freshness deadline. Active
+requires an explicit Active shape, its declared source policy, and
+plant-safety checks. HOLD requires an explicit HOLD shape and a live lease.
+ESTOP requires explicit intent and an enrolled ESTOP-capable grant, but it does
+not inherit the live-lease or source requirements. The final remote-admission
+result is:
+
+$$
+A_k=B_k\land G_k(m_k).
+$$
+
+This factorization describes command admission. An authenticated, authorized
+ESTOP can separately change a retained local latch before its final stream
+verdict when the detailed target permits that order. Such a latch does not make
+a rejected command admitted.
+
+Let $u_k^{\mathrm{remote}}$ be the remote software selection:
+
+$$
+u_k^{\mathrm{remote}}=
+\begin{cases}
+u_k^\star, & A_k\land m_k=\mathrm{Active}, \\
+r_k^{\mathrm{mode}}(m_k),
+  & A_k\land m_k\in\{\mathrm{Hold},\mathrm{ESTOP}\}, \\
+\bot, & \neg A_k.
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+$r_k^{\mathrm{mode}}$ comes from the installed plant profile. The remote frame
+does not supply its values. Let $R_k$ be a separate body-local restrictive
+decision, and let $r_k^{\mathrm{local}}$ be its installed action. The selected
+software action is:
+
+$$
+u_k^{\mathrm{sel}} =
+\begin{cases}
+r_k^{\mathrm{local}}, & R_k, \\
+u_k^{\mathrm{remote}}, & \neg R_k.
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+Rejected bytes cannot select or parameterize $r_k^{\mathrm{local}}$. A rejection
+and a local restrictive decision can coexist, but their evidence remains
+separate. The symbol $\bot$ means no new software action. It is not a numeric
+zero, a HOLD, an ESTOP, or a physical-effect claim.
+
+Let the deployment select a bounded plant-disturbance set $\mathcal{W}_p$.
+For $w_k^p\in\mathcal{W}_p$, let $u_k^p$ be the actual plant input and let $P$
+be the deployment plant transition. NCP does not prove the equality
+$u_k^p=u_k^{\mathrm{sel}}$. The next state is:
+
+$$
+x_{k+1} = P(x_k,u_k^p,w_k^p).
+$$
+
+The next accepted sample $y_{k+1}$ closes the cycle only when it is attributable
+to the body disposition and the intervening deployment transition. If no later
+state is sampled, the system has command evidence but no feedback evidence. A
+read-only observer can receive a bounded observation correlated to $\Sigma_k$.
+Observer loss cannot change $A_k$, $u_k^{\mathrm{sel}}$, or body restrictive
+state.
+
+### Closed-loop timing
+
+Distributed publisher clocks are not subtracted. A receiver can define latency
+only from events sampled on one body clock incarnation. Let
+$t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B$, $t_{\mathrm{admit},k}^B$,
+$t_{\mathrm{terminal},k}^B$, and $t_{\mathrm{observe},k}^B$ be the retained body
+ticks for sample ingress, software admission, terminal disposition, and local
+observer release. Then:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+L_{\mathrm{admit},k}
+  &= t_{\mathrm{admit},k}^B-t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B, \\
+L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}
+  &= t_{\mathrm{terminal},k}^B-t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B, \\
+L_{\mathrm{observe},k}
+  &= t_{\mathrm{observe},k}^B-t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B.
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+Each duration exists only when both named events were retained on the same clock
+incarnation. Each valid duration is finite and nonnegative. Let
+$d_{\mathrm{source},k}^B$ be the receiver-derived exclusive source deadline on
+that clock. Let $D_{\mathrm{admit}}$ be a selected admission budget. Ordinary
+Active admission requires:
+
+$$
+t_{\mathrm{admit},k}^B < d_{\mathrm{source},k}^B
+\quad\land\quad
+L_{\mathrm{admit},k}\leq D_{\mathrm{admit}}.
+$$
+
+The strict first inequality preserves the source deadline. Equality is expired.
+The second inequality is a selected local budget. For an admitted action with a
+later terminal disposition, event order also requires:
+
+$$
+0\leq L_{\mathrm{admit},k}
+\leq L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}.
+$$
+
+A deployment can select a separate $D_{\mathrm{disposition}}$ and test
+$L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}\leq D_{\mathrm{disposition}}$. Neither software
+interval is a physical-effect measurement. The equations do not prove network
+determinism, device response, or physical control performance.
+
+`ControlStatus.loop_latency_ms` has a narrower boundary. For the current
+reference loop it measures local elapsed time from tick entry through final
+safety governance:
+
+$$
+L_{\mathrm{local},k}
+=t_{\mathrm{governed},k}^{C}-t_{\mathrm{tick},k}^{C}.
+$$
+
+The superscript $C$ identifies the controller process clock. This value excludes
+transport-slot admission, network delivery, body admission, device effect, and
+observation. It must never be presented as
+$L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}$ or a physical-effect interval.
+
+Period jitter is retained as a distribution, not one average. Given a selected
+reference period $\Delta_{\mathrm{ref}}>0$:
+
+$$
+J_k = \Delta_k-\Delta_{\mathrm{ref}}.
+$$
+
+Qualification reports bounded counts, minimum, maximum, quantiles, misses, and
+the sampling method. A local percentile from one machine is not a universal
+deadline.
+
+### Stability boundary
+
+NCP preserves the information required to assess timing and causality. It does
+not make a controller stable. For a continuous linear plant:
+
+$$
+\dot x(t)=A_cx(t)+B_cu(t),
+$$
+
+zero-order hold over one measured period $\Delta$ gives:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+A_d(\Delta) &= e^{A_c\Delta}, \\
+B_d(\Delta) &= \int_0^{\Delta} e^{A_c\theta}B_c\,d\theta.
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+For state feedback $u_k=Kx_k$, the nominal discrete transition is:
+
+$$
+x_{k+1}=\left(A_d(\Delta_k)+B_d(\Delta_k)K\right)x_k.
+$$
+
+Define $M(\delta)=A_d(\delta)+B_d(\delta)K$. Under the stated linear,
+unsaturated, delay-free assumptions, a fixed-period nominal system is
+asymptotically stable if and only if:
+
+$$
+\rho\!\left(M(\Delta)\right)<1,
+$$
+
+where $\rho$ is spectral radius. Pointwise spectral-radius checks do not in
+general prove stability when $\Delta_k$ varies. Let $\mathcal{D}$ be the selected
+set of admissible periods. One sufficient arbitrary-switching certificate is:
+
+$$
+\begin{gathered}
+P=P^\mathsf{T}\succ0,\qquad Q=Q^\mathsf{T}\succ0,\\
+M(\delta)^\mathsf{T}P M(\delta)-P\preceq-Q
+\quad\text{for every }\delta\in\mathcal{D}.
+\end{gathered}
+$$
+
+With $V(x)=x^\mathsf{T}Px$, this certificate gives:
+
+$$
+V(x_{k+1})-V(x_k)\leq-x_k^\mathsf{T}Qx_k<0
+\quad\text{for }x_k\neq0.
+$$
+
+This common quadratic certificate is sufficient and can be conservative. A real
+qualification must cover the selected plant and controller, measured delay and
+jitter set, saturation, estimator, quantization, packet loss, fallback
+transitions, and nonlinearities. NCP does not infer either stability condition
+from a successful exchange.
+
+### Current executable evidence
+
+The reference `ncp-zenoh` loopback test now executes twelve commands between
+thirteen exact samples. Each command requires the exact source stream position,
+the matching live session, a new action-stream position, a matching unit, a
+content-addressed reference plant-profile check, and a declared numerical plant
+transition. The causally next sample must show a strict reduction in reference
+error. A separate read-only tap observes all thirteen samples. This is local
+reference-path evidence only. The test does not retain the complete publication
+coordinate $\Sigma_k$, implement the authenticated body predicate $A_k$, or
+produce a durable body disposition receipt.
+
+The installed Engram-to-Crebain-to-Prisoma loop is **NOT RUN**. Engram has a
+native-1.0 migration in progress. Crebain and Prisoma currently expose wire-0.8
+surfaces. Their dependency-ready migration and installed qualification tasks are
+blocked. NCP does not invent a translator, lease, effect receipt, or live peer to
+make this diagram appear complete.
+
