+\section{Why NCP exists when MUSIC exists}
+
+MUSIC is an API for MPI-based neuronal simulators to exchange event and continuous data during
+one coordinated multi-simulation.  Participating programs link the MUSIC library, share its
+communicator, and advance simulated time through runtime ticks.  See the
+\href{https://ebrains.eu/tools/music}{EBRAINS MUSIC tool page} and the
+\href{https://nest-simulator.readthedocs.io/en/stable/tutorials/music_tutorial/music_tutorial_3.html}{NEST
+MUSIC tutorial}.  NCP addresses a different boundary.  It defines a versioned network contract
+among a neural service, a body, a simulator, and read-only observers that can be deployed,
+restarted, upgraded, and secured independently.
+
+NCP adds explicit wire identity, authenticated roles, session generations, authority leases,
+stream positions, deadlines, idempotent lifecycle operations, plant-profile binding, and bounded
+failure behavior.  It complements MUSIC, ROS, and Zenoh.  It does not replace their scheduling,
+device, or transport functions.  NEST remains behind an adapter or gateway instead of acquiring
+NCP transport logic in its scientific kernel.
+
+\NcpFigure{H}{0.92\linewidth}{purpose-light.pdf}{Why NCP remains necessary beside MUSIC, transports, and device stacks.}
+
+The figure is read by ownership, not by product order.  MUSIC can coordinate a MUSIC-aware
+simulation behind an adapter.  Zenoh can carry NCP traffic, and a robotics stack can integrate a
+device.  Neither relationship supplies NCP role authority, body admission, or physical-effect
+evidence.  The adapter preserves the boundary explicitly instead of copying protocol definitions
+into a simulator kernel or consumer project.
+
+\clearpage
+\section{Causal closed-loop system model}
+
+\NcpFigure{H}{0.98\linewidth}{closed-loop-light.pdf}{Causal NCP feedback model and its evidence boundary.}
+
+A loop is causal only when a body disposition contributes to a plant transition that appears in a
+later sample.  A sensor-to-command exchange is one forward path, not a complete feedback cycle.
+The figure has five numbered steps: sample, decide, correlate, admit or restrict, then advance.
+
+Let $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$ be the control-cycle index.  Let $x_k\in\mathcal{X}$ be
+deployment-owned plant state at body sample time $\tau_k^B$.  The superscript $B$ identifies one
+body-owned monotonic clock incarnation.  The realized sample period is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-period}
+  \Delta_k=\tau_{k+1}^B-\tau_k^B>0.
+\end{equation}
+The value $\Delta_k$ becomes known only when sample $k+1$ exists.  Both ticks must be finite and
+belong to the same clock incarnation.  A zero or negative value is a clock or ordering fault.  It
+cannot establish a fast loop.
+
+Let the deployment select a bounded sensor-disturbance set $\mathcal{W}_s$.  For
+$w_k^s\in\mathcal{W}_s$, let $h$ be the installed sampling function.  The observed value is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-sample}
+  y_k=h(x_k,w_k^s).
+\end{equation}
+The deployment defines $h$, channel units, arities, ranges, and plant profile.  NCP preserves
+those declarations.  It does not invent a unit conversion or repair a malformed sample.
+
+The sample owns the full exact equality coordinate and its wire projection
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-source-coordinate}
+\begin{aligned}
+  \Lambda_k&=(r,s,g,\ell_k,p_k,f_k,a_k,e_k),\\
+  \Sigma_k&=(\Lambda_k,q_k,c_k),\\
+  \pi_{\mathrm{source}}(\Sigma_k)&=(e_k,q_k).
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+Here, $\Lambda_k$ is the exact prepared source declaration.  It binds authority realm $r$, logical
+session $s$, live generation $g$, literal source route $\ell_k$, verified publisher principal
+$p_k$, frame class and layout $f_k$, installed authorization/security activation digest $a_k$,
+and declared epoch $e_k$.  The position $q_k$ is strictly increasing inside that declaration, and
+$c_k$ is the retained canonical publication digest.  The projection $\pi_{\mathrm{source}}$ is the
+exact \texttt{StreamPosition} carried by \texttt{CommandFrame.source}.  The prepared action
+declaration identifies $\Lambda_k$.  The body resolves $(\Lambda_k,q_k)$ to one retained record
+and compares $c_k$ and typed content exactly.  The projection alone is not the full coordinate.
+Arrival time, queue order, a latest value, or a nearby timestamp cannot substitute for the record.
+This proves the command's declared source identity.  It does not prove that opaque controller code
+used that sample to calculate the proposal.
+
+Let $E$ map one admitted typed sample to controller input.  Let $z_k$ be controller state.  Let
+$F$ be one prepared controller transition, and let $D$ decode controller state into a proposal.
+The controller cannot use the future realized interval $\Delta_k$ at decision $k$.  Define its
+available interval as
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:controller-available-interval}
+  \delta_k^{\mathrm{ctl}}=
+  \begin{cases}
+    \Delta_{\mathrm{init}},&k=0,\\
+    \tau_k^B-\tau_{k-1}^B=\Delta_{k-1},&k\geq1,
+  \end{cases}
+\end{equation}
+where $\Delta_{\mathrm{init}}>0$ is prepared and bound to the controller profile.  Both body
+ticks in the second branch must be finite and belong to the same clock incarnation.  A missing
+prior interval, a clock cut, or an unavailable checked difference rejects or invokes the
+installed restrictive policy.  It does not cause the controller to guess a period.  The causal
+controller transition is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:controller-transition}
+  \hat y_k=E(y_k),\qquad
+  z_{k+1}=F(z_k,\hat y_k,\delta_k^{\mathrm{ctl}}),\qquad
+  u_k^\star=D(z_{k+1}).
+\end{equation}
+The controller can be a reflex, a NEST-backed model, or another qualified implementation.
+Protocol success does not validate $F$, $D$, a paper reproduction, or a trained model.  A
+closed-loop command carries \texttt{source}$=\pi_{\mathrm{source}}(\Sigma_k)$, names the prepared
+binding for $\Lambda_k$, and carries matching route/session members.  Its command stream remains
+distinct.
+
+The body-owned common remote-admission predicate is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-admission}
+\begin{aligned}
+B_k={}&A_k^{\mathrm{raw}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{auth}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{manifest}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{wire}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{session}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{actor}}\\
+&\land A_k^{\mathrm{security}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{profile}}
+\land A_k^{\mathrm{executor}}.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The terms require bounded structure, verified ingress, an installed default-deny manifest,
+accepted wire identity, current session, verified actor, current security state, the plant
+profile, and reserved executor capacity.  Every common term must hold.  Unknown or missing terms
+are false.
+
+Let $\chi_k$ be the declared source policy.  Its gate is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-source-policy}
+A_k^{\mathrm{source}}(\chi_k)=
+\begin{cases}
+\mathrm{true},&\chi_k=\mathrm{open\mbox{-}loop},\\
+\text{exact retained correlation to }\Sigma_k,&\chi_k=\mathrm{source\mbox{-}bound}.
+\end{cases}
+\end{equation}
+The closed mode gate is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-mode-gate}
+G_k(m_k)=
+\begin{cases}
+A_k^{\mathrm{stream}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{grant,active}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{shape,active}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{lease}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{source}}(\chi_k)\land A_k^{\mathrm{safety}},&m_k=\mathrm{Active},\\
+A_k^{\mathrm{stream}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{grant,hold}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{lease}}
+ \land A_k^{\mathrm{shape,hold}},&m_k=\mathrm{Hold},\\
+A_k^{\mathrm{stream}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{grant,estop}}\land A_k^{\mathrm{shape,estop}},
+ &m_k=\mathrm{ESTOP},\\
+\mathrm{false},&\text{otherwise}.
+\end{cases}
+\end{equation}
+Each grant includes its body-issued freshness deadline.  Active requires its explicit shape,
+declared source policy, and safety checks.  HOLD requires an explicit shape and a live lease.
+ESTOP requires explicit intent and an enrolled ESTOP-capable grant, but it inherits neither
+source nor lease.
+Thus
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-mode-admission}
+A_k=B_k\land G_k(m_k).
+\end{equation}
+This result describes command admission.  A qualified ESTOP can separately change a retained
+local latch before its final stream verdict.  That latch does not admit a rejected command.
+
+The remote software selection is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-remote-selection}
+u_k^{\mathrm{remote}}=
+\begin{cases}
+u_k^\star,&A_k\land m_k=\mathrm{Active},\\
+r_k^{\mathrm{mode}}(m_k),&A_k\land m_k\in\{\mathrm{Hold},\mathrm{ESTOP}\},\\
+\bot,&\neg A_k.
+\end{cases}
+\end{equation}
+The installed plant profile supplies $r_k^{\mathrm{mode}}$.  The remote frame does not.  Let
+$R_k$ be an independent body-local restrictive decision with installed action
+$r_k^{\mathrm{local}}$.  The software selection is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-action}
+  u_k^{\mathrm{sel}}=
+  \begin{cases}
+    r_k^{\mathrm{local}},&R_k,\\
+    u_k^{\mathrm{remote}},&\neg R_k.
+  \end{cases}
+\end{equation}
+Rejected bytes cannot select or parameterize $r_k^{\mathrm{local}}$.  A rejection and a local
+restriction can coexist with separate evidence.  The symbol $\bot$ means no new software action.
+It means neither numeric zero nor physical effect.
+
+Let the deployment select a bounded plant-disturbance set $\mathcal{W}_p$.  For
+$w_k^p\in\mathcal{W}_p$, let $u_k^p$ be the physical plant input and let $P$ be the deployment
+transition.  The protocol boundary and next state are
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-effect-boundary}
+\mathrm{NCP}\nvdash\bigl(u_k^p=u_k^{\mathrm{sel}}\bigr).
+\end{equation}
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-plant}
+  x_{k+1}=P(x_k,u_k^p,w_k^p).
+\end{equation}
+The later accepted sample $y_{k+1}$ closes the cycle only when it is attributable to the body
+disposition and the intervening deployment transition.  Without it, the system has command
+evidence but no feedback evidence.  A read-only observer can receive an observation correlated
+to $\Sigma_k$.  Observer loss cannot change $A_k$, $u_k^{\mathrm{sel}}$, or restrictive state.
+
+\subsection{Closed-loop timing}
+
+Distributed publisher clocks are not subtracted.  Let $t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B$,
+$t_{\mathrm{admit},k}^B$, $t_{\mathrm{terminal},k}^B$, and
+$t_{\mathrm{observe},k}^B$ be body-clock ticks for sample ingress, software admission, terminal
+disposition, and local observer release.  The three receiver-local durations are
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-latencies}
+\begin{aligned}
+L_{\mathrm{admit},k}&=t_{\mathrm{admit},k}^B-t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B,\\
+L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}&=t_{\mathrm{terminal},k}^B-t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B,\\
+L_{\mathrm{observe},k}&=t_{\mathrm{observe},k}^B-t_{\mathrm{sample},k}^B.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+Each duration exists only when both named events were retained on the same clock incarnation.
+Each valid duration is finite and nonnegative.  Let $d_{\mathrm{source},k}^B$ be the
+receiver-derived exclusive source deadline on that clock.  Let $D_{\mathrm{admit}}$ be a selected
+admission budget.  Ordinary Active admission requires
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-deadline}
+  t_{\mathrm{admit},k}^B<d_{\mathrm{source},k}^B
+  \quad\land\quad
+  L_{\mathrm{admit},k}\leq D_{\mathrm{admit}}.
+\end{equation}
+Equality in the first comparison is expired.  The second comparison is a selected software
+budget.  For an admitted action with a later terminal disposition, event order also requires
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-disposition-order}
+  0\leq L_{\mathrm{admit},k}\leq L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}.
+\end{equation}
+A deployment can select a separate $D_{\mathrm{disposition}}$ and test
+$L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}\leq D_{\mathrm{disposition}}$.  Neither software interval is a
+physical-effect measurement.  The equations do not prove device response or physical control
+performance.
+
+\texttt{ControlStatus.loop\_latency\_ms} has a narrower boundary.  For the reference loop it
+measures local controller-process time from tick entry through final governance:
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-local-latency}
+  L_{\mathrm{local},k}
+  =t_{\mathrm{governed},k}^{C}-t_{\mathrm{tick},k}^{C}.
+\end{equation}
+The superscript $C$ identifies one controller-process clock.  The interval excludes
+transport-slot admission, network delivery, body admission, body disposition, device effect, and
+observation.  It must never be presented as $L_{\mathrm{disposition},k}$ or a physical-effect
+interval.
+
+Given a selected reference period $\Delta_{\mathrm{ref}}>0$, sample-period jitter is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:loop-jitter}
+  J_k=\Delta_k-\Delta_{\mathrm{ref}}.
+\end{equation}
+Qualification reports bounded sample count, minimum, maximum, quantiles, misses, and method.  A
+local average or percentile from one machine is not a universal deadline.
+
+\subsection{Stability boundary}
+
+NCP preserves information needed to assess timing and causality.  It does not make a controller
+stable.  For a continuous linear plant,
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:continuous-plant}
+  \dot x(t)=A_cx(t)+B_cu(t).
+\end{equation}
+Zero-order hold over a measured period $\Delta$ gives
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:discrete-plant}
+  A_d(\Delta)=e^{A_c\Delta},\qquad
+  B_d(\Delta)=\int_0^\Delta e^{A_c\theta}B_c\,d\theta.
+\end{equation}
+For state feedback $u_k=Kx_k$, the nominal discrete transition is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:closed-loop-transition}
+  x_{k+1}=\left(A_d(\Delta_k)+B_d(\Delta_k)K\right)x_k.
+\end{equation}
+A fixed-period nominal system uses
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:stability-matrix-family}
+  M(\delta)=A_d(\delta)+B_d(\delta)K.
+\end{equation}
+Under the stated linear, unsaturated, delay-free assumptions, it is asymptotically stable if and
+only if
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:stability-condition}
+  \rho\!\left(M(\Delta)\right)<1,
+\end{equation}
+where $\rho$ is spectral radius.  Pointwise checks do not in general prove stability when
+$\Delta_k$ varies.  Let $\mathcal{D}$ be the selected admissible-period set.  One sufficient
+arbitrary-switching certificate is
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:common-lyapunov}
+\begin{gathered}
+P=P^\mathsf{T}\succ0,\qquad Q=Q^\mathsf{T}\succ0,\\
+M(\delta)^\mathsf{T}PM(\delta)-P\preceq-Q
+\quad\text{for every }\delta\in\mathcal{D},\\
+V(x_{k+1})-V(x_k)\leq-x_k^\mathsf{T}Qx_k<0
+\quad\text{for }x_k\neq0,\qquad V(x)=x^\mathsf{T}Px.
+\end{gathered}
+\end{equation}
+This common quadratic certificate is sufficient and can be conservative.  A real qualification
+covers the selected plant and controller, measured delay and jitter set, saturation, estimation,
+quantization, loss, fallback transitions, and nonlinearities.  NCP does not infer either
+stability condition from a successful exchange.
+
+\subsection{Executable and ecosystem evidence}
+
+The reference \texttt{ncp-zenoh} loopback test executes twelve commands between thirteen exact
+samples.  Each command requires exact source equality, a new action-stream position, a matching
+unit, a content-addressed reference plant-profile check, and a declared numerical plant
+transition.  The causally next sample must show a strict reduction in reference error.  A separate
+read-only tap observes all thirteen samples.  This is local reference-path evidence only.  It does
+not implement the authenticated complete body predicate $A_k$ or produce a durable body
+disposition receipt.
+
+The installed Engram-to-Crebain-to-Prisoma loop is \textbf{NOT RUN}.  Engram has a native-1.0
+migration in progress.  Crebain and Prisoma expose wire-0.8 surfaces.  Their dependency-ready
+migration and installed qualification tasks are blocked.  NCP does not invent a translator,
+lease, effect receipt, or live peer to make the diagram appear complete.
+
+\clearpage
